                <pb facs="0707" n="583" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg583"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">881.00/857: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harvey</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-10-31T10:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 31,
                                1923—10 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 10:15
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>477. Through the Foreign Office I have learned informally that the question of
                    abolishing the diplomatic agencies at Tangier has not been brought up before the
                    Conference and for that reason the British Government has not had it under
                    consideration. It seems to the Foreign Office, however, that a natural
                    consequence of the complete internationalization of Tangier would be, by its
                    broad nature, to eliminate diplomatic envoys to Morocco to be stationed at
                    Tangier, though of course other officials of the foreign powers would be
                    represented in the administration. It was pointed out to me in addition that no
                    power that was signatory to the convention of Algeciras is bound by the
                    decisions of the present conference and that they would be given full
                    opportunity to approve or disapprove if and when a formula is adopted. I have
                    been assured that the British Government is endeavoring to obtain complete and
                    independent internationalization. In opposition the French will attempt to
                    create a nominal internationalization really under French control.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harvey</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
